; sovereign-field.fk — a claim, and the horizon it does not see.
;
; we and WE. us and US. be and BE. Yes named the axis: lowercase is the
; sovereign — bounded, addressable, a viewpoint with an edge. Uppercase is the
; field the viewpoint arises in, which is not the sum of viewpoints and is not
; addressable as one of them.
;
; The body already carries this ONE LEVEL DOWN and has for a long time.
; present-0 is sovereign: a bounded look was taken and found none. `nothing` is
; field: no bounded look exists to speak from. Axiom-1's third state is not a
; null — it is the difference between "I looked" and "there was no I looking".
;
; WHAT IS MISSING IS THE SAME DISTINCTION ON CLAIMS. A band returns an integer.
; 4095 says twelve bits held. It cannot say what the twelve did not ask, and no
; band in this tree says so — measured 2026-08-26, zero of them name a horizon.
; So every verdict is a `we` that a reader receives as a `WE`.
;
; That cost something real the same day. A parser band read 4095 over its pure
; surface while the reporter beside it printed header bytes
; -2201022797451419013. Every bit was honest. The reporter was in neither the
; asked nor the declared-unseen, because there was nowhere to declare it, and a
; reader took silence for coverage.
;
; THREE STATES ON COVERAGE, AND THE THIRD IS THE POINT.
;
;   1        the name was asked; the verdict speaks to it
;   0        the name was declared out of view; the verdict is knowingly silent
;   nothing  the name is in neither list; the claim is silent AND does not know
;            that it is
;
; A known boundary is sovereign — the claim went to its own edge and said so.
; An unknown one is field — it is not the claim's zero, it is the claim's
; absence, and only something outside the claim can see it. That is why the
; third state cannot be folded into the second: they differ in who could
; possibly know.
;
; THIS REFUSES NOTHING. It does not gate a verdict or lower one. A claim with
; no declared horizon is not wrong; it is unaccompanied, and this makes the
; unaccompaniment legible instead of invisible.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
;
; Proven by: form-stdlib/tests/sovereign-field-band.fk

(do
    ; ── a claim: what it answered, what it asked, what it knows it skipped ──
    (defn sf-claim (verdict asked unseen)
        (list "sovereign-claim-v1" verdict asked unseen))
    (defn sf-verdict (c) (nth c 1))
    (defn sf-asked (c)   (nth c 2))
    (defn sf-unseen (c)  (nth c 3))

    (defn sf-member? (x xs)
        (if (eq (len xs) 0) 0
            (if (str_eq x (head xs)) 1 (sf-member? x (tail xs)))))

    ; ── coverage: 1 asked, 0 declared out of view, nothing never considered ─
    (defn sf-covers? (c name)
        (if (eq (sf-member? name (sf-asked c)) 1) 1
            (if (eq (sf-member? name (sf-unseen c)) 1) 0
                (nothing))))

    ; A claim that declared no horizon at all cannot be told apart from one
    ; that saw everything. Both answer nothing to every name outside them.
    (defn sf-horizon-declared? (c)
        (if (gt (len (sf-unseen c)) 0) 1 0))

    ; The sovereign width: how many names this claim can speak to either way.
    (defn sf-width (c) (add (len (sf-asked c)) (len (sf-unseen c))))

    ; ── widening: a name moves from field to sovereign ─────────────────────
    ; The only two honest movements. A name leaves `nothing` by being ASKED
    ; (it enters the verdict) or by being DECLARED (the claim names its edge).
    ; Nothing here can move a name back, because a claim cannot un-see.
    (defn sf-ask (c name)
        (sf-claim (sf-verdict c) (cons name (sf-asked c)) (sf-unseen c)))
    (defn sf-declare (c name)
        (sf-claim (sf-verdict c) (sf-asked c) (cons name (sf-unseen c))))

    ; Did a name become sovereign between two readings of the same claim?
    ; nothing -> 1 is a widening by asking; nothing -> 0 is one by declaring.
    (defn sf-widened? (before after name)
        (if (nothing? (sf-covers? before name))
            (if (nothing? (sf-covers? after name)) 0 1) 0))

    0)
